f the House's most contested seats. Ashley Hinson's retirement to run for Senate leaves a Republican-leaning district without an incumbent in a national climate that favors Democrats, and both parties have poured attention and money into the race. The first debate in early October centered on affordability, immigration and the Iran war, with the candidates trading personal attacks.\n\nExpert raters mostly call the race a tossup, though Cook and Inside Elections tilt Republican while the Economist and FPO lean Democratic. The only recent poll, a Democratic-sponsored July survey, showed James ahead by a single point. Mitchell holds a modest cash advantage, but Democratic outside groups have spent heavily against him and his work history has drawn scrutiny. Prediction markets price Democrats slightly ahead, leaving a genuine coin flip with a faint Democratic edge.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.5595, "margin": 0.94}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.5585, "margin": 0.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6246, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.619, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.5799, "margin": 1.8}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.557, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.6133, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.6185, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.6098, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.5891, "margin": 1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.6068, "margin": 1.2}]}